our friend’s truck is triggering when he just gets a faster green at a normal light. The fact that these exist and are so different underscores that standard lights don’t use weight. ### 5. If weight were the trigger, an empty truck would behave differently from a loaded one As I mentioned before, your truck‑driver friend can confirm that his empty and loaded truck get the same response. If weight mattered, the 80,000‑lb loaded truck would presumably get an ever faster green than the 35,000‑lb empty one. That doesn’t happen. The metal frame is the constant, and that’s what triggers the loop. ### 6. The historical origin of the “strip” or “cut” in the pavement Those rectangular, diamond, or circular cuts are the undeniable fingerprint of an inductive loop. A scale would require a smooth, flush‑mounted metallic plate (like at a grocery store cart return) or a thick rubber‑encased sensor strip running across the lane (for piezo). The cuts are literally the trench through which the wire was pulled. That’s how you can spot a loop on any street. --- I hope this helps explain why I’m so certain. The idea feels right because we instinctively link “heaviness” with “bigger vehicle,” but the technology in the ground is simply listening for metal, not measuring mass. It’s perhaps the most pervasive and persistent myth in traffic engineering, so you’re in very good company finding it hard to believe. If you’d like to dig deeper, a quick search for “how inductive loop traffic detectors work” from any transportation agency will show the same explanation every time.
